{"name":"Cranberry Whip","hex":"#8e4541","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cranberry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shrub belonging to the section Vaccinium sect. Oxycoccus of the genus Vaccinium.","The edible red berry of that shrub."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of the intense red colour of a cranberry."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cranberry"},{"term":"whip","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A lash; a pliant, flexible instrument, such as a rod (commonly of cane or rattan) or a plaited or braided rope or thong (commonly of leather) used to create a sharp \"crack\" sound for directing or herding animals.","The same instrument used to strike a person or animal for corporal punishment or torture."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To hit with a whip.","To hit with any flexible object."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/whip"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cranberry","blocks":[{"text":"Cranberries are a group of evergreen dwarf shrubs or trailing vines in the subgenus Oxycoccus of the genus Vaccinium. Cranberries are low, creeping shrubs or vines up to 2 meters (7 ft) long and 5 to 20 centimeters in height; they have slender stems that are not thickly woody and have small evergreen leaves. The flowers are dark pink. The fruit is a berry that is larger than the leaves of the plant; it is initially light green, turning red when ripe. The raw berry is edible and has a tart taste due to the low sugar content and high acidity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Cranberry_bog.jpg/330px-Cranberry_bog.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cranberry"},{"title":"Whip","blocks":[{"text":"A whip is a blunt weapon or implement used in a striking motion to create sound or pain. Whips can be used for flagellation against humans or animals to exert control or strength through pain compliance or fear of pain, or be used as an audible cue through the distinct whipcrack effect. Whips are commonly used on horses to give subtle cues as tapping. The portion of the whip used for striking is generally either a firm rod designed for direct contact, or a flexible line requiring a specialized swinging motion. The former is easier and more precise, the latter offers longer reach and greater force. Some varieties have an extended stock section in addition to the line."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Riding_crop.jpeg/330px-Riding_crop.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whip"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Cranberries20101210.jpg/500px-Cranberries20101210.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Cranberries20101210.jpg/500px-Cranberries20101210.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Raw cranberries","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACranberries20101210.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Cranberry_harvest%2C_Kingston_Massachusetts.jpg/500px-Cranberry_harvest%2C_Kingston_Massachusetts.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Cranberry_harvest%2C_Kingston_Massachusetts.jpg/500px-Cranberry_harvest%2C_Kingston_Massachusetts.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cranberry harvest","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACranberry_harvest%2C_Kingston_Massachusetts.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/%D0%96%D1%83%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B0.jpg/500px-%D0%96%D1%83%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikip